    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The algorithm"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "let i = 0;\nwhile (i < nums.length) {\n  const correctIndex = nums[i];            // for range 0..N-1\n  if (nums[i] !== nums[correctIndex]) {\n    [nums[i], nums[correctIndex]] = [nums[correctIndex], nums[i]];\n  } else {\n    i++;\n  }\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Read the loop carefully: `i` only advances when the current slot is settled. Otherwise we swap and check the same index again, because the swap brought in a new value that may also be misplaced."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why it is O(N), not O(N²)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A `while` with a conditional increment looks dangerous. But every swap places **at least one value into its final position**, permanently. There are N values, so there can be at most N swaps in the entire run. Add at most N increments of `i`, and the total is bounded by 2N."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why the comparison uses values, not indices"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "`nums[i] !== nums[correctIndex]` compares *values*, where `nums[i] !== correctIndex` might seem more natural. The value-based test handles duplicates gracefully: if the target slot already holds the right value, swapping would loop forever."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Watch the range convention"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For a range of 1 to N, the target index is `nums[i] - 1` rather than `nums[i]`. Getting this wrong gives out-of-bounds errors immediately — which is at least a loud failure. Always write down the range before coding."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The signal to use it"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Look for: numbers in a fixed range 1..N or 0..N−1, a question about missing or duplicate values, and a constraint demanding O(1) space. All three together means cyclic sort."
    }
  ],
